completion:
The proposition that only wrongdoers need to be concerned about the ease of official surveillance in the electronic age is both insightful and, in many ways, accurate—but it misses a crucial point about the broader impact of pervasive surveillance. While it is true that only individuals who have committed illegal or unethical acts have something to fear from authorities monitoring their digital activity, the reality is that the mere existence of widespread surveillance creates an atmosphere of constant monitoring for everyone. This can lead to self-censorship, erosion of privacy, and a chilling effect on free thought and expression—even for those who have done nothing wrong. 

In an electronic age where our emails, messages, purchases, and even our movements can be tracked, the psychological and social consequences are real. People may alter their behavior, avoid certain topics, or limit their associations out of fear that they might inadvertently attract unwanted attention. This is not just a concern for the guilty; it threatens the democratic values of openness, dissent, and innovation. 

Moreover, the line between what is legally monitored and what is not can become increasingly blurred as technology advances. While the proposition focuses on wrongdoers, society should also consider the ethical responsibility to protect innocent citizens from unnecessary intrusion. In the end, official surveillance is a double-edged sword: it deters crime, but it also demands vigilance from all of us to ensure it does not become a tool for oppression or social control. The real worry should be not just about wrongdoers, but about the future of liberty for everyone in a world where our every click can be watched." }

