While the proposition suggests that businesspeople and manufacturers hold greater importance than writers and artists in society, I believe this perspective overlooks the essential role creativity and imagination play in driving progress and enriching human life. Writers and artists are the architects of culture, the storytellers who shape values and inspire change. They challenge the status quo, provoke thought, and give voice to new ideas that can ultimately transform industries and economies. 

On the other hand, businesspeople and manufacturers are crucial for turning ideas into tangible goods and services, making them accessible to the public and fueling economic growth. However, without writers and artists to interpret, critique, and elevate these products, the full potential of innovation may remain unexplored or disconnected from the human experience. 

In a healthy society, both roles are indispensable. The businessperson and manufacturer provide the infrastructure and means of production, while writers and artists imbue those products with meaning, emotion, and vision. To prioritize one group over the other risks stifling the very creativity and cultural dynamism that sustain thriving economies in the long run. True progress happens when both are valued equally and work in harmony." }

