completion:
I think both charity and social security have important roles to play in supporting the genuinely disadvantaged, but they serve different purposes and reach different groups. Social security systems provide a safety net for people who have contributed to society through work over time, ensuring a baseline level of support for those who may not be able to work due to age, disability, or other circumstances. This system is efficient, scalable, and helps prevent extreme poverty for large segments of the population.

On the other hand, charity—whether from individuals, nonprofits, or private organizations—can offer targeted help to people who fall through the cracks of systemic support. It can address immediate needs, fill gaps that social security doesn't reach (like the homeless or recent immigrants without work history), and sometimes foster innovation in aid and community support.

In an ideal system, social security would provide the foundation, while charity would supplement it for those who are most in need but still outside the safety net. Relying solely on charity is impractical for a society's scale and sustainability, but dismissing it entirely ignores the vital role of direct, compassionate aid. So, I believe a combination of both approaches is the most effective way to genuinely help the disadvantaged." }

