completion:
I believe that the proposition 'It is a waste of time to try to rehabilitate some criminals' requires a nuanced response. While it's undeniable that rehabilitation does not always result in complete transformation or that some individuals may never fully reintegrate into society productively, dismissing the entire concept of rehabilitation as a waste of time is overly simplistic. 

Rehabilitation programs aim to address the root causes of criminal behavior—such as trauma, addiction, or social disadvantage—giving offenders the opportunity to change and contribute positively to society. For many, these programs have proven effective, reducing recidivism rates and enabling individuals to rebuild their lives. However, it's also true that not every rehabilitation effort will succeed for every individual, and some may require different approaches or may not be suited for reentry at all.

Rather than labeling the effort as a waste, it's more constructive to advocate for a smarter, more targeted approach to rehabilitation. Society should invest in identifying which offenders are most likely to benefit and tailor interventions accordingly, while also recognizing the limits of any one-size-fits-all solution. Ultimately, the goal should be to give people a second chance when it is reasonable to do so, recognizing both the potential for change and the need for realistic expectations. We must not conflate the challenge of success with the futility of effort." }

