completion:
I believe that the proposition, 'Those who are able to work and refuse the opportunity should not expect society’s support,' holds a fair amount of merit. In a functioning society, the value of work is not just economic—it’s also social and personal. Choosing not to work, especially when one is capable, can have broader implications for both the individual and society as a whole. 

On one hand, individuals have the right to pursue their own paths for personal reasons—health, family, passion, or simply a desire for leisure. However, expecting society to subsidize a lifestyle that doesn’t contribute to the common good can strain public resources and create unfair burdens on those who do contribute. 

Ultimately, I think the principle should be balanced: people who choose not to work for valid personal reasons should not be expected to rely solely on society for support, but they also shouldn’t be denied access to basic social safety nets that recognize human dignity and the reality that life can present unexpected challenges. Society’s role is to support all members in times of need, while also encouraging productive participation where it’s desired. It’s a nuanced position that recognizes both freedom and responsibility." }

