completion:
I believe that the proposition, 'Those who are able to work and refuse the opportunity should not expect society’s support,' holds a fundamentally sound perspective. In any functioning society, the principle of mutual responsibility is essential for maintaining economic stability and social cohesion. When individuals choose not to participate in the workforce, even when they are capable, they forgo the contributions that work brings—economic productivity, social interaction, and personal development. Society, in turn, relies on the labor of its productive members to meet collective needs and provide essential services. 

Granting support to those who refuse meaningful employment can create incentives for others to disengage as well, potentially leading to a cycle of dependency and reduced societal productivity. While there may be compassionate reasons for exceptions—such as health issues or personal circumstances—it is important that such support remains targeted and temporary, rather than becoming a normalized expectation for all able-bodied but unwilling individuals. 

In summary, encouraging self-reliance and valuing the contributions of working citizens strengthens both individuals and society as a whole. Those who are able and choose not to work should understand that, in exchange for the privileges and safety nets provided by society, they are expected to contribute in ways that align with its functioning." }

