completion:
As a 65-year-old, I have spent much of my adult life long past the days of mandatory school attendance. While I understand the rationale behind enforcing compulsory attendance—ensuring children receive basic education and do not fall behind in developing essential skills—I believe that making classroom attendance compulsory for all may not be the best approach for everyone. 

There are many reasons why a person might not benefit from traditional classroom learning. Some students thrive in self-directed environments, others have learning disabilities or special needs that are better addressed through alternative education methods, and some simply have lives and responsibilities that make regular school attendance impractical. Forcing attendance could lead to disengagement, increased stress, or even resentment in students who would be better served by tailored learning experiences.

Instead of a one-size-fits-all compulsory system, I believe schools should offer a variety of educational pathways. This way, students can choose the learning environment that best suits their abilities and circumstances. Education should be about growth and empowerment, not just about showing up for a set number of hours. Compulsory attendance ignores the diversity of human potential and the realities of modern life. Let us focus on creating inclusive, flexible systems that allow all children to learn in the way that works for them." }

