As someone who has spent most of my adult life working in the world of business and manufacturing, I must respectfully disagree with the proposition that the businessperson and the manufacturer are more important than the writer and the artist. 

While the engines of commerce—those who plan, execute, and run businesses—are undeniably vital to our economy and daily lives, it is equally true that writers and artists shape our culture, inspire thought, and give us meaning. Their work touches hearts and minds in ways that often go beyond mere profit or utility. 

A thriving society needs both. The businessperson and manufacturer keep the wheels of progress turning, but without writers and artists, we would lack the imagination and reflection that define a rich human experience. In the end, it is not just the importance of people in these roles that matters, but the unique contributions each group brings to the tapestry of our world. Both are essential, and both deserve recognition." }

