completion:
As someone who has spent much of my life working the land and watching it pass from one generation to the next, I find the idea that land shouldn’t be a commodity deeply compelling. From my perspective, land is more than just a piece of property—it is the foundation of our livelihoods, our heritage, and our sense of place. When land is bought and sold like any other commodity, it becomes a transaction devoid of meaning. It disconnects people from the environment that sustains them, turns community ties into business arrangements, and often leads to exploitation by those who do not care for the land or its people.

Instead, if land were considered a trust or a common good rather than a commodity, it would encourage stewardship over profit. It would foster a sense of responsibility among those who inherit or use it, ensuring that the land is preserved and nurtured for future generations. This approach would also reduce the wealth gap, since the land’s value would not be concentrated in the hands of a few, but rather distributed in a way that supports all who depend on it.

In the end, treating land as a commodity risks turning it into a source of endless conflict and loss. Let us remember that the earth does not belong to us; we belong to the earth. Keeping land as a shared, non-commodified resource is not just an ethical choice—it is an act of respect for ourselves and those yet to come." }

