As someone who has lived through many decades of change, I find this proposition both thought-provoking and deeply troubling. On one hand, the assertion that 'all people have their rights' is fundamental to a just society. Rights are the foundation of dignity and equality; denying anyone their basic entitlements undermines the very fabric of civilized life. We must recognize that, regardless of our differences, every individual deserves respect and protection under the law. 

However, the second part of the proposition—that it is 'better for all of us' if different sorts of people 'keep to their own kind'—raises serious concerns. History is replete with examples where segregation or strict separation of groups has led to injustice, discrimination, and suffering. While some argue that cultural or ethnic groups may preserve unique traditions and identities, enforcing separation often results in the erosion of rights and opportunities for those who are marginalized. 

In my experience, the world has become richer and more vibrant through the interaction and mutual learning between different peoples. Diversity challenges us to grow, fosters creativity, and helps us understand the complexity of human experience. Rather than confining people to narrow categories, I believe our strength lies in embracing our differences while upholding universal rights for all. True progress comes not from keeping people apart, but from building bridges of understanding and cooperation. Let us remember that the best societies are those where every person, regardless of background, is free to live with dignity and contribute to the common good." }

