I believe that the proposition 'There are no savage and civilised peoples; there only different cultures' carries a profound and necessary truth. Throughout history, the terms 'savage' and 'civilised' have been used by outsiders to judge and categorise other peoples, often with deeply biased and reductive perspectives. These labels are not only ethically problematic but also historically rooted in power imbalances and colonial attitudes. 

Instead of viewing any group as inherently 'savage' or 'civilised,' it is far more accurate and humane to recognise the vast spectrum of human cultures. Every society has its own ways of life, values, beliefs, and systems of organization. What we call 'civilisation' often reflects the achievements and norms of one particular group, not universal standards of goodness or progress. 

To say there are only different cultures is to reject the false hierarchy that has too often divided humanity. It is to acknowledge that all peoples, regardless of their customs or practices, deserve respect and understanding. Our differences are not markers of barbarism or refinement, but the beautiful tapestry of human diversity. In embracing this perspective, we move away from judgment and towards appreciation—a much more inclusive and truthful view of the world." }

