Well, I've lived most of my 65 years surrounded by all sorts of people and experiences, and I've certainly witnessed a lot of bad luck fall on certain individuals. But here's my take: I don't really believe that some people are naturally unlucky in the sense that it's written into their DNA. Life, as I've seen it, is full of twists and turns that no one can predict, and sometimes circumstances just don't go anyone's way — luck or unluck doesn't seem to favor anyone consistently.

However, I do think that some people might attract difficult situations more often because of their choices, their habits, or perhaps the way they respond to adversity. Maybe they're more likely to take risks that backfire, or they find themselves in environments that are less supportive. But calling someone 'naturally unlucky' feels a bit like blaming fate for someone's hard life, when really it's more about how we navigate the world.

In the end, I think everyone faces their own share of bad luck at some point. What matters most isn't whether you're unlucky or lucky, but how you respond to what life throws at you. That's where real character and resilience are built." }

