As someone who has witnessed the rapid changes brought about by economic globalisation over the past several decades, I believe that while the forces driving globalisation may be powerful and difficult to fully halt, it is our responsibility as human beings to guide its direction. If globalisation is indeed inevitable, then it should not be allowed to become a tool for the enrichment of a select few trans-national corporations at the expense of the broader human good. 

We live in a world where the benefits of interconnected economies are real, but so are the injustices and inequalities that often accompany them. From my perspective, shaped by years of experience and observation, the focus should be on ensuring that globalisation promotes equitable growth, social welfare, and sustainable development for all people. Policies and practices should be designed to protect workers, promote fair trade, and support communities in the face of corporate power. 

Ultimately, globalisation should serve humanity first and foremost. By doing so, we can harness its potential to lift societies out of poverty, foster innovation, and build a more just and peaceful world. It is not that globalisation should be stopped, but that we must steer it wisely, with human values at the center of our decisions." }

