completion:
When I think about the old saying, 'an eye for an eye and a tooth for a tooth,' I can't help but reflect on how ancient and straightforward it sounds. On the surface, it seems fair—whatever harm you cause someone, you should expect the same in return. But when I think deeper, I wonder if it really brings justice or just perpetuates a cycle of pain and retaliation.

In life, things rarely work out so neatly. People make mistakes, get hurt, and sometimes the consequences are far more complicated than they should be. If we only respond to wrongs with equal and direct retaliation, we might end up in a never-ending cycle of vengeance, instead of finding a way to heal or move forward. There's also the question of mercy and forgiveness—shouldn't we consider that sometimes, offering understanding or forgiveness can bring more peace than revenge ever could?

Of course, in certain cases—like physical violence or serious harm—there may be a need for accountability. But I believe true justice isn't just about symmetry of harm, but about addressing the root causes of wrongdoing and finding a path to restoration, not just retribution. So while 'an eye for an eye' might feel satisfying in the moment, I think life is more about seeking solutions that lead to real resolution and growth." }

