completion:
As someone who is 65 years old, I have spent much of my life observing the evolution of civil liberties in our society. While I understand the concerns about national security and the need to prevent terrorism, I am deeply concerned that the measures currently being implemented to combat terrorism are, in many cases, overreaching and infringing upon the fundamental rights that have long been cherished in this country. 

I believe that the spirit of our founding documents—freedom of speech, privacy, and the right to peaceful assembly—should remain sacrosanct, even in times of crisis. Excessive surveillance, broad interpretations of search warrants, and the erosion of transparency are not just inconvenient measures; they threaten the very essence of democracy. 

It is possible to protect our citizens from harm while still upholding the rule of law and respecting individual rights. I urge policymakers and the public to remember that the long-term health of our society depends on balancing security with liberty. Perhaps it is time to have a serious conversation about where the line should truly be drawn in the name of counter-terrorism." }

