completion:
As someone who has spent most of my life observing society and the changes it undergoes, I feel compelled to share my thoughts on the question of whether punishment should take precedence over rehabilitation in criminal justice. Throughout my years, I have witnessed people who have made mistakes in their youth go on to become productive, law-abiding citizens. Yet, too often, the focus seems to remain on punishing them for what they've done in the past, rather than helping them address the root causes of their actions and guiding them toward a better path forward. 

I believe rehabilitation should be the cornerstone of our criminal justice system. True justice isn't just about retribution; it's about giving individuals the opportunity to learn, to change, and to contribute positively to society once again. When we prioritize punishment above all, we risk creating a cycle of recidivism and perpetuating suffering. Rehabilitation offers hope, healing, and a chance at redemption. It recognizes that people can grow and that society benefits when former offenders become good citizens. 

Of course, there are cases where punishment is necessary as a deterrent or to protect the public from serious offenders. But the goal should be to balance both approaches: hold wrongdoers accountable while also investing in their rehabilitation. In the end, a justice system that focuses more on rehabilitation not only serves the victims by reducing repeat crimes but also serves the offenders by giving them a path out of the cycle of addiction, poverty, or anger that often leads to crime in the first place. That, to me, is the true measure of justice." }

