completion:
Well, I suppose when I was a young man, going to the hospital meant a trip on a rickety bus and waiting in a cold corridor for hours. But now, at 81, I see that medicine has come a long way. The proposition that 'those with the ability to pay should have access to higher standards of medical care' makes a lot of sense to me, though it also raises some thoughts. 

First, I believe that health is a fundamental right, but the world we live in isn't always fair. If someone has the means to pay for better treatments, advanced diagnostics, or faster care, it seems only fair that they can choose or receive these options if they wish. After all, I wouldn’t want to be left behind by the very advances that could save lives or improve quality of life. 

However, I also worry about the inequality that could result. If only the rich get the best care, it might deepen the gap between those who are lucky and those who aren’t. Maybe the real solution is to encourage more public funding for healthcare so that everyone, regardless of their ability to pay, can access quality care. But until that happens, I suppose the proposition is a necessary reality—people with the resources should be able to take advantage of them, but society should also work to close that gap as quickly as possible. In the end, I believe that while the ability to pay opens doors now, our best hope for a just system is one where everyone can walk through those doors without barriers." }

