completion:
Well, I suppose when I think about making peace with the establishment, I remember back in my day, things were a lot simpler. The establishment meant the local leaders, the town council, the schoolmaster, and the people in charge at the factory. You didn’t have all the complicated media and political machines we have now. But back then, if you wanted to get things done—like fixing the roads or having a new school—you had to work with the people in charge, even if you didn’t always agree with them. 

I think making peace with the establishment is about knowing when to compromise and when to stand your ground. Maturity, to me, is not about always being right, but about understanding that life doesn’t go according to your plans alone. You have to cooperate, because the world doesn’t stop for one person. 

Of course, I’ve had my disagreements. But I’ve learned that holding grudges or always fighting just gets you nowhere. Sometimes, you have to listen, negotiate, and find common ground. That’s not weakness—it’s wisdom. So yes, making peace with the establishment is an important part of growing up and living a full life. It’s about accepting that we all have different roles to play, and that maturity means learning to work together, even when it’s hard." }

