Well, I suppose when I think about the proposition that 'the only social responsibility of a company should be to deliver a profit to its shareholders,' I can't help but recall my own life and the world around me. I've seen times when businesses closed down because the owners wanted to maximize profits, and then the local shops, cafes, and even small jobs disappeared. People I know lost their livelihoods because someone else's focus on profit took precedence over the well-being of the community. 

While making a profit is certainly important and necessary for a company to survive and grow, I've also witnessed how companies that cared about their communities—by supporting education, charity, or improving local infrastructure—often built a loyal customer base and long-term success. I believe that in today's world, ignoring everything else but profit can lead to a society where people are left behind. 

So, while I understand the argument for profit as the sole responsibility, from my perspective, I think a company's true social responsibility also includes looking after its employees, supporting local communities, and contributing to the greater good. After all, a healthy society benefits everyone, including the shareholders in the long run. Perhaps the best companies are those that balance profit with responsibility." }

