Well, after eighty-one years of watching schools and children come and go, I've seen a lot of changes and not a few regrets. Compulsory classroom attendance, on the surface, seems like a good idea—ensuring that kids get some basic education. But I've also seen children who were forced to attend, not because they loved learning, but because rules were rules. Some were bored, others were troubled, and a few just didn't fit in the mold. 

I believe that forcing every child to attend class, regardless of their circumstances or interests, doesn't take into account the individual needs of each child. There are many ways to learn and grow, and sometimes the classroom isn't the right environment for everyone. Perhaps if learning was something voluntary and tailored to a child's pace and style, more would truly enjoy and benefit from it. 

Of course, I understand the importance of basic education for society to function, but making it compulsory for all may push some children away from curiosity and discovery. Education should inspire, not just require. So, my opinion is that schools shouldn't make classroom attendance compulsory for everyone. Instead, we should find ways to encourage and support all children in learning in ways that suit them best." }

