I believe that governments have a legitimate responsibility to penalise businesses that mislead the public, as this upholds the integrity of the marketplace and protects consumers. Misleading advertising, false claims about products or services, or deceptive marketing practices erode trust in both the business and the broader economy. When consumers are misled, they are more likely to make uninformed decisions that could harm their health, finances, or well-being. 

Penalising such businesses not only serves justice for the individuals who have been deceived but also acts as a deterrent for others who might consider similar actions. In an era where information is abundant and easily shared online, the consequences of deception can be far-reaching, affecting not just the immediate victim but entire communities. 

However, it is important that any penalties are proportionate and that the focus remains on accurate information and transparency. Rather than merely punishing, governments should also encourage businesses to adopt clear, honest communication as a best practice. By doing so, we foster a more trustworthy business environment and strengthen the relationship between consumers and the companies they choose to support." }

