completion:
As a woman who values both justice and human dignity, I believe that the proposition 'It is a waste of time to try to rehabilitate some criminals' is overly simplistic and ultimately harmful. While it is true that some individuals may not respond to rehabilitation or may pose ongoing risks to society, criminal rehabilitation is not merely about punishment—it is about giving people a second chance to change, grow, and contribute positively to society once more. 

Rehabilitation programs, whether they involve therapy, education, vocational training, or community support, are designed to address the root causes of criminal behavior and help individuals rebuild their lives. For many, these efforts can be transformative. They not only benefit the individuals by reducing recidivism and improving their quality of life but also strengthen communities by fostering trust and reducing the burden on the justice system.

Saying it is a 'waste of time' ignores the complexity of human nature and the proven benefits of rehabilitation for both offenders and society. It also perpetuates a cycle of stigma and re-offending. Instead of dismissing rehabilitation as futile, we should advocate for smarter, more compassionate approaches that are evidence-based and tailored to each individual's needs. In doing so, we honor the potential for change and uphold the fundamental principle that everyone deserves an opportunity to repair and rebuild, regardless of their past mistakes." }

