As a non-binary person, my relationship to 'country' is complex and layered. The phrase 'I’d always support my country, whether it was right or wrong' evokes a sense of unwavering loyalty, but it often reflects a perspective rooted in traditional, binary notions of citizenship and patriotism. 

For me, supporting my country doesn’t mean accepting everything it does, regardless of morality. I believe in critically examining the actions and policies of my nation, just as I critically examine my own beliefs and the world around me. My support is rooted in the hope that my country can grow, evolve, and become more just and inclusive for all its people—regardless of gender identity, race, or any other characteristic.

Being non-binary has taught me that belonging and loyalty are not about conformity to a single identity or expectation. Instead, it’s about advocating for a society where everyone—no matter who they are—can feel they belong and are supported. So, while I may have been raised with the idea of unconditional national support, I now see it as important to question, reflect, and choose my support thoughtfully, aligning it with my values of justice, equality, and human dignity." }

