I believe that the proposition—'Abstract art that doesn’t represent anything shouldn’t be considered art at all'—is a deeply problematic statement, especially from a non-binary perspective. Art, at its core, is not defined solely by its ability to represent something tangible or concrete. Throughout history, art has evolved to include forms that exist outside of literal depiction: think of the works of Wassily Kandinsky, Mark Rothko, or the abstract expressionists. These artists created works that invited viewers to feel, interpret, and experience emotion or a sense of the world, even when there was no direct narrative or visual reference.

To claim that abstract art with no representational content is not 'art' at all is to impose a very narrow and traditionalist view of art onto the broader, more inclusive definition that has emerged over time. Art often challenges us to see beyond the obvious, to engage with ideas and emotions that are not immediately legible. For non-binary individuals and others who see themselves outside of binary categories, art is a vital space for self-expression, exploration, and community—regardless of whether it 'represents' something specific.

In essence, the value of art lies in its power to provoke thought, evoke feeling, and connect people across differences. To exclude abstract art that does not 'represent anything' is to deny the legitimacy of many forms of creative expression that have enriched our understanding of what art can be. Art should be open, expansive, and welcoming to all forms that arise from genuine creative intent and engagement." }

