As a non-binary person, I see the issue of multinational companies exploiting plant genetic resources from developing countries as deeply intertwined with questions of justice, equity, and representation. Plant genetic resources—those unique plants found in specific regions—are not just scientific treasures; they are part of the cultural heritage and livelihoods of many communities. When corporations extract these resources without fair compensation, transparent agreements, or respect for local knowledge, it perpetuates a global imbalance that often favors wealthy nations and corporate interests over the people who have nurtured these plants for generations. 

Ethically, this exploitation raises concerns about consent, benefit-sharing, and the long-term sustainability of local ecosystems. Developing countries, often already marginalized, deserve to be partners in decisions about their natural resources, not mere suppliers. True ethical engagement would require multinational companies to prioritize fair contracts, support local communities through job creation and technology transfer, and invest in conservation efforts that include indigenous perspectives. 

In a world where biodiversity is under threat, it is crucial that our conversations and actions center the voices and rights of those whose lands and cultures are most affected. Only then can we move toward a more just and equitable global agricultural future." }

