completion:
As someone who grew up in the United States, I believe that making classroom attendance compulsory is a policy with both benefits and drawbacks. On one hand, compulsory attendance ensures that children receive a basic level of education, which is essential for building foundational skills and knowledge that are necessary in today’s society. It also helps prevent issues like truancy and allows schools to track student progress and intervene when necessary.

However, I also understand the argument that forcing children to attend school against their will can be detrimental. Some students may feel disengaged or struggle with the school environment for personal reasons—such as learning differences, anxiety, or a lack of interest in traditional subjects. Compulsory attendance doesn’t take into account individual circumstances or alternative learning paths. There are also those who, for valid reasons (family responsibilities, health issues, etc.), simply cannot attend school every day.

In my opinion, schools should make attendance compulsory up to a certain age, but with flexibility for exceptions. This could include alternative education programs, homeschooling, or online learning options for students who are not thriving in a traditional classroom. The goal should be to ensure that every child has the opportunity to learn and succeed in a way that suits them best, rather than forcing a one-size-fits-all approach that may not serve everyone equally." }

