completion:
From a Danish perspective, the question of rehabilitating criminals is one that carries both cultural nuance and practical considerations. In Denmark, as in many European countries, there is a strong belief in rehabilitation and second chances. The Danish criminal justice system is designed not only to punish but also to offer support for those who have made mistakes, with an emphasis on understanding the root causes of criminal behavior and providing education, vocational training, and psychological support.

While it is true that some individuals may never fully grasp the consequences of their actions or may pose ongoing risks to society, research and experience in Denmark show that many offenders can be positively transformed through rehabilitation. Programs such as the 'Rehabilitation and Support' (Rehabilitering og Støttelse) initiatives have proven effective in reducing recidivism and helping former criminals reintegrate into society as productive members of the community.

To label rehabilitation as a 'waste of time' ignores the complex realities of crime and human nature. For every person who remains in prison, there may be another who, with the right support, avoids a life of crime and contributes positively to society. Therefore, rather than dismissing rehabilitation as futile, we should continue to refine and strengthen these efforts, ensuring they are evidence-based and accessible to all who need them. In Denmark, we believe that giving people a chance to change is not only humane but also beneficial for society as a whole." }

