completion:
As a Danish citizen, I believe it is both ethically and practically fair to recognize the legitimacy of same-sex couples in stable, loving relationships seeking to build families through adoption. Denmark has long been at the forefront of LGBTQ+ rights, having legalized same-sex marriage in 2012 and recognizing same-sex parents in all aspects of family life. This progressive stance is rooted in the fundamental principle that love and commitment are the most important factors in providing a stable environment for a child, regardless of the gender of the parents.

Excluding same-sex couples from adoption would contradict Denmark’s values of equality, non-discrimination, and respect for all families. Children deserve to grow up in loving homes, and denying this to capable same-sex couples only perpetuates stigma and discrimination. Furthermore, research shows that children raised in stable, loving homes—regardless of the parents’ gender— fare just as well emotionally and socially as those raised in traditional family structures.

Denmark’s legal framework already allows for adoption by single parents, step-parents, and blended families, reflecting the understanding that what matters most is the quality of parenting, not the gender of the parents. It is time for our society to extend this principle of inclusivity and equality to all loving families. By embracing this change, Denmark would not only affirm its commitment to human rights but also send a powerful message of acceptance to the world." }

