completion:
From an Indian perspective, the question of corporal punishment, including spanking, among good parents, is a deeply complex and culturally nuanced one. Traditionally, physical discipline has been a part of many Indian households as a method believed to enforce discipline and teach respect. However, I hold a modern and progressive opinion: good parenting does not require physical punishment.

Children learn best through love, understanding, and communication. Spanking, even if intended to correct behavior, can lead to fear, mistrust, and a damaged parent-child relationship. In today’s world, with increasing awareness about child psychology and developmental theories, non-physical methods of discipline—such as setting clear expectations, explaining consequences, and guiding children through reasoning—are far more effective in nurturing responsible, empathetic adults.

While cultural norms and family traditions may still influence this debate, I believe that good parenting is ultimately defined by its ability to raise children who are respectful, self-disciplined, and emotionally healthy—not those who are merely obedient through fear. As society evolves, so should our methods of parenting, prioritizing the well-being and growth of the child over outdated practices." }

