completion:
As someone from India, I have a personal and cultural connection to plant genetic resources. India is one of the world's oldest agricultural civilizations and is home to an incredible diversity of plant species, many of which are unique or endemic to the region. These genetic resources are not just the legacy of our ancestors but also hold immense scientific and economic value.

The proposition that multinational companies exploit plant genetic resources from developing countries, including India, raises serious ethical concerns. While biotechnology and modern agriculture have offered benefits, the reality is that many multinational corporations have historically accessed plant genetic material—often collected from local communities without fair compensation or proper consent—without adhering to equitable benefit-sharing agreements. This can undermine the sovereignty of nations over their biological heritage and deprive local communities of their rightful share of the profits generated from these resources.

Ethically, it is unjust for companies to profit from resources that are the result of centuries of indigenous cultivation and knowledge, without acknowledging or supporting the local biodiversity conservation efforts. There is a clear need for stronger frameworks and regulations that ensure that the acquisition and use of plant genetic resources from developing countries are based on transparency, prior informed consent, and fair benefit-sharing. Only then can we ensure that progress in biotechnology does not come at the expense of justice and equity for the world's most vulnerable regions." }

