completion:
As someone from Brazil, I have witnessed firsthand the profound impact compulsory classroom attendance can have on children's lives, especially in our diverse and sometimes underprivileged communities. While the intention behind enforcing attendance is to ensure that children receive basic education—a fundamental right in Brazil according to the Federal Constitution—I believe that making attendance compulsory for all may not always serve the best interests of every child. 

There are many reasons why a child might struggle to attend school regularly: poverty, family responsibilities, health issues, or even a lack of appropriate infrastructure in some regions. Forcing attendance in such circumstances could lead to stress, dropouts, or even further marginalization. Instead, a more flexible approach that allows for exceptions in certain situations could better address the real needs of students. 

Education is vital, but so is respecting the realities of life for many families. Compulsory attendance, without consideration for these complexities, risks turning school into a barrier rather than a gateway to opportunity. A balanced policy—one that prioritizes the holistic well-being of children—would be wiser. Education should be accessible, not just mandatory." }

