completion:
As a Brazilian, I have a deep connection to the topic of protectionism in trade. While globalization has brought many benefits to our economy, such as access to new markets and technology, it has also exposed us to unfair competition from countries with lower production costs. Sometimes, foreign goods that are produced at a lower price can flood our markets, harming local industries and workers who rely on them for their livelihoods. 

From my perspective, protectionism is sometimes necessary as a tool to protect emerging industries that are still developing their competitive edge. By imposing tariffs or quotas on certain imported products, we can give local businesses the time and space they need to grow, innovate, and eventually compete globally. This approach is not about closing the door to the world, but about building a stronger foundation for our own economy in the long run. 

However, I believe that protectionism must be used judiciously and not as a permanent solution. It should be a temporary measure to support strategic sectors, while we work on improving our productivity and competitiveness. Ultimately, the goal should be to create a fairer and more balanced international trading system where all countries can thrive." }

