I believe that both charity and social security have important roles in supporting the genuinely disadvantaged, but they serve different purposes and address different aspects of inequality. Social security systems are designed to provide a safety net for people who have contributed to society through work, ensuring a baseline level of support regardless of individual circumstances. This approach can help reduce poverty for large segments of the population who rely on steady income and are vulnerable to economic shocks.

On the other hand, charity—whether from individuals, nonprofits, or community groups—can offer targeted assistance to those in the most acute need, often filling gaps that social security alone cannot reach. Charitable organizations can respond quickly to emergencies, support marginalized groups who may not qualify for social security (such as the homeless or undocumented immigrants), and innovate new ways to help people in distress.

Rather than seeing charity and social security as mutually exclusive, I think they are complementary. A robust approach would combine the broad, reliable protection of social security with the flexibility and responsiveness of charitable giving. This combination can create a more just and effective safety net for all who are disadvantaged, ensuring no one is left behind simply because of the structure of their life or circumstances." }

