On the other hand, charity—whether from individuals, nonprofits, or faith-based organizations—can offer targeted help to those who may not qualify for social security for various reasons (like lack of work history, immigration status, or being outside the formal economy). Charity can also innovate, respond to immediate crises, and fill gaps that social security alone cannot address.

In my view, a robust approach would combine both: social security provides stability and fairness for a broad section of society, while charity ensures that no one is left behind because of circumstances beyond their control or the limitations of the social security system. Relying solely on charity risks creating inconsistent support and placing an unsustainable burden on individuals and organizations. So, I believe a mixed strategy that strengthens both systems is the most effective way to help the genuinely disadvantaged." }

