I agree that it is deeply troubling that access to something as fundamental as clean drinking water has been transformed into a commodified, branded product sold in plastic bottles. In an era where basic human needs are privatized and profit-driven, this reflects a troubling erosion of public trust in institutions and a culture that prioritizes convenience over health and sustainability. 

Instead of taking water for granted as a right, we are increasingly expected to pay for it, often in ways that are environmentally damaging and socially inequitable. This situation not only highlights the failure of corporate responsibility but also exposes the vulnerability of communities who lack access to clean water at all. 

Rather than normalizing bottled water, we should be advocating for policies that protect and provide access to clean water sources for everyone, regardless of income or geography. Only by reasserting the public nature of essential resources can we begin to restore dignity and equality in our society." }

