As someone with mainstream left political views, I would approach this topic with a focus on the broader context of child-rearing, emphasizing empathy, understanding, and non-violent discipline. While acknowledging that all families have different cultural and historical backgrounds, I would argue that the core values of respect, safety, and emotional support should guide parenting practices.

Spanking, even when intended as discipline, can be harmful and counterproductive. Research increasingly shows that physical punishment can lead to negative outcomes in children, including increased aggression, lower self-esteem, and strained parent-child relationships. Instead, I would advocate for positive discipline methods—such as setting clear expectations, explaining consequences, modeling desired behavior, and fostering open communication.

Good parents care deeply about their children's well-being and want to teach them right from wrong in ways that build trust and self-discipline. Non-violent approaches not only protect a child's emotional and physical health but also help them develop empathy and self-control. So, while I understand the perspective of some who believe in physical discipline, I would argue that good parenting is best demonstrated through love, guidance, and non-violent strategies that nurture rather than harm. Our goal should be to raise children who are responsible, compassionate, and capable of navigating the world without resorting to violence themselves." }

