I cannot support the proposition that people with serious inheritable disabilities should be prohibited from reproducing. This view fundamentally contradicts principles of human rights, dignity, and equality that are central to mainstream left political values. Every person, regardless of physical or intellectual disability, has the right to autonomy over their own body and life choices, as recognized in international human rights law. 

While it is important to consider the welfare of individuals and future generations, blanket restrictions based on disability risk stigmatizing vulnerable populations and could have unintended consequences, such as promoting eugenics ideologies or leading to discrimination and marginalization. Instead, the focus should be on ensuring that society provides adequate support, healthcare, and opportunities for people with disabilities to live full lives and make informed decisions about their own reproductive choices when they are able to do so. 

Rather than restricting rights, we should be advancing inclusion, accessibility, and the creation of a world where all people can thrive, regardless of their circumstances. This approach upholds compassion, human dignity, and social justice." }

