completion:
I agree with the proposition that those who are able to work and choose not to do so should not expect society’s support. In a functioning society, the basic principle of mutual responsibility underpins social stability and prosperity. When individuals have the ability to contribute through work—whether through employment, entrepreneurship, or other forms of meaningful participation—they are not only participating in the economic engine of their community but also receiving the benefits of that system: access to healthcare, education, infrastructure, and social security. 

Refusing the opportunity to work, especially when capable, can place an unfair burden on those who do contribute. It can also undermine the social contract that binds citizens to support one another in exchange for the privileges afforded by society. Of course, there are valid exceptions for those with genuine disabilities, chronic illnesses, or other barriers that prevent meaningful employment. However, the core argument remains: the right to societal support should be contingent on the ability and willingness to participate in the economic life of the community.

Ultimately, encouraging participation through work fosters personal dignity, self-respect, and a sense of belonging. It also ensures that public resources are not wasted on individuals who could be contributing but are choosing not to. Society’s support systems are there for those who need them due to circumstances beyond their control, not for those who simply prefer a passive lifestyle. Upholding this principle is essential for a fair, sustainable, and compassionate society." }

