completion:
While it is true that access to clean water has improved dramatically over centuries, the current trend of bottling and branding plain drinking water strikes me as an unfortunate regression. Our society has made incredible progress in public health, ensuring that clean water is available to most people in developed nations. However, the commercialization of this essential resource seems to prioritize profit over public good. 

Bottled water exists largely due to marketing, convenience, and misplaced consumer preference rather than necessity. In many urban and rural areas, tap water is safe, affordable, and environmentally responsible. By treating water as just another consumer product with a label and brand, we risk diminishing the sense of community responsibility and environmental stewardship that should accompany such a vital resource.

Instead of seeing bottled water as a symbol of modern progress, it should serve as a reminder of where we still have work to do in ensuring basic needs are accessible and not commodified. Let’s encourage policies and public awareness that reinforce the value of clean, affordable, and sustainable water from our taps." }

