completion:
As someone who values personal responsibility and the importance of individual choice, I would argue that making classroom attendance compulsory is a policy that should be re-examined. Compulsory attendance enforces a one-size-fits-all approach to education, which can inadvertently stifle the development of independent thinking and life skills in students. Many young people, especially those from diverse backgrounds or facing personal challenges, may benefit from alternative paths that better suit their needs and circumstances. 

Rather than mandating attendance, schools could focus on providing flexible educational opportunities that encourage students to pursue learning at their own pace. This could include part-time education, apprenticeships, or self-directed study programs, all of which combine practical experience with academic growth. By allowing students to choose when and how they engage with education, we empower them to take ownership of their learning journey and foster a more resilient, self-motivated generation. 

Compulsory attendance also fails to account for the realities of modern life—such as work, family responsibilities, or health issues—that often prevent young people from attending school every day. Instead of punishing those who cannot fit the traditional model, we should adapt our educational systems to be more inclusive and supportive of all young people's aspirations and limitations. Ultimately, education should be about equipping individuals with the tools to succeed in life, not about forcing everyone into the same rigid schedule. Let's create a system that values individual potential and real-world experience as much as academic knowledge." }

