completion:
As someone who holds mainstream right-leaning views, I would approach this topic by considering the complexities of social divisions in modern society. While it's true that class divisions—based on economic status, education, and access to resources—often play a significant role in shaping people's experiences and worldviews, I believe that nationality also profoundly influences social identities and group dynamics. 

Nationality can create strong bonds of shared history, culture, language, and sometimes even economic systems (such as in national economies or labor markets). People's loyalties, identities, and even their economic opportunities can be deeply tied to their national context. For example, the economic structure in one country may offer more upward mobility than another, or cultural narratives can be shaped heavily by national history.

That said, I agree that class divisions are real and can cut across national lines, leading to intra-national disparities. However, I would argue that the extent to which people are ultimately divided depends on the circumstances of their lives. In many parts of the world, national identity remains a more powerful unifying or dividing force than class, especially when it comes to broader societal attitudes, political allegiances, and collective goals. Ultimately, I see class and nationality as overlapping factors—class can affect how people experience national identity, and national context can shape the opportunities that determine class. But to say that people are divided more by class than by nationality overlooks the unique ways in which national belonging shapes communities and identities." }

