completion:
Economic globalisation is, in many respects, an unstoppable force driven by technological advancement, interconnected markets, and the realities of a single planet with interdependent economies. While it is true that globalisation is largely inevitable, this does not absolve us of the responsibility to shape its direction. The primary purpose of global economic systems should be to benefit all of humanity—not just the privileged few who control the largest trans-national corporations (TNCs). 

These corporations, though powerful, are ultimately subject to the will of the global citizenry. If we allow globalisation to be driven solely by profit motives, we risk deepening inequality, eroding national sovereignty, and exacerbating social and environmental crises. Instead, we must advocate for policies and governance structures that ensure global trade and investment serve broader public good objectives: fair wages, environmental sustainability, access to essential goods and services, and social protections. 

In other words, even if globalisation cannot be stopped, we can—and must—decide how it should function. The onus is on governments, citizens, and international institutions to steer it toward outcomes that promote human flourishing over corporate aggrandizement." }

