completion:
I firmly believe that land should not be treated as a mere commodity to be bought and sold on the open market. Land is fundamentally different from other forms of property—it is the foundation of our society, providing not just a physical space for human activity but also a vital link to our environment, heritage, and sense of place. When land is commodified, it becomes subject to profit motives that can undermine its social, ecological, and cultural value. 

Allowing unrestricted buying and selling of land can lead to the displacement of communities, the erosion of local character, and the prioritization of short-term gains over long-term sustainability. It also risks concentrating ownership in the hands of powerful entities, further exacerbating inequality and undermining democratic control over resources that affect us all.

Instead, land should be recognized as a public trust or held in common by the community or governed by responsible stewardship frameworks. This approach ensures that the needs of current and future generations are prioritized over profit, preserves the integrity of our landscapes, and upholds the dignity of where we all live. Treating land as anything less than a sacred responsibility is to betray the very foundation upon which our well-being depends." }

