completion:
I firmly believe that the claim that multinational companies are unethically exploiting plant genetic resources from developing countries is an overgeneralization that ignores the complex realities of international biotechnology and corporate governance. While it is true that some companies have historically extracted value from the genetic resources of other nations without fair compensation or respect for indigenous knowledge, many corporations today operate under frameworks such as the Convention on Biological Diversity (CBD) and the Nagoya Protocol, which aim to ensure equitable benefit-sharing and recognition of local communities.

However, the system is not perfect. In practice, some multinational agribusinesses may still fail to adequately compensate local farmers or provide transparent access to the value of the resources they use. This can be exacerbated by weak enforcement of regulations and lack of transparency in patenting and licensing processes. But to broadly label all multinational companies as 'unethical exploiters' is misleading—it overlooks the progress made in partnerships and the growing emphasis on sustainability and responsible research and development.

Ultimately, the solution lies not in blanket accusations but in strengthening international oversight, enforcing benefit-sharing agreements, and empowering local communities to have greater control over the use and commercialization of their genetic resources." }

