completion:
Economic globalisation is, in many respects, an unstoppable force. The interconnectedness of markets, supply chains, and technological platforms means that nations and corporations cannot retreat behind national borders to avoid the realities of a modern, information-based world. To accept this inevitability is not to surrender to it, but to demand that we shape its trajectory in a way that prioritises human well-being over the bottom line of a few powerful corporations. 

Trans-national corporations, while undeniably influential, are profit-seeking entities whose primary loyalty is to shareholders, not to the public good or to the broader societal interests of the nations they operate in. Globalisation, when guided by the interests of these corporations, often results in policies that favour short-term gains, exploit labour, and undermine local economies. 

Instead, we must advocate for globalisation that is regulated and inclusive. This means strengthening the role of governments in ensuring fair competition, enforcing robust labour standards, promoting transparency, and investing in public goods such as education, healthcare, and infrastructure. Only by placing humanity at the centre of global economic systems can we prevent the worst excesses of unregulated capitalism and ensure that globalisation ultimately serves to uplift societies as a whole, not just the privileged few at the top of the corporate hierarchy." }

