completion:
I firmly believe that the principle 'the ablest should pay' is fundamentally at odds with a just and equitable society. The proposition that 'those with the ability to pay should have access to higher standards of medical care' dangerously implies that health outcomes are a commodity to be bought and sold. This approach perpetuates inequality by allowing the wealthy to secure superior medical treatment while the less fortunate are left with substandard care, simply because they cannot afford it.

Instead of rewarding ability to pay, our society should be structured to ensure that all citizens, regardless of income, have access to the highest possible standard of medical care. This is not only a moral imperative but also a practical necessity for public health and social stability. When basic healthcare is left to market forces without regulation, it creates a system where the healthy and well-off are further insulated from the sick and struggling. This exacerbates health disparities and undermines social cohesion.

True fairness means that society invests in healthcare as a right, not a privilege. Those who can afford extras should not be the only ones receiving the best treatment. Rather, we should reform our healthcare systems to remove financial barriers and ensure that quality care is universally accessible. This way, we foster a stronger, healthier, and more united nation where no one is left behind due to economic status." }

